## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**.  
MapLibre GL JS itself does not generate contours, but the **`maplibre-contour`** helper library does the heavy lifting for you:

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| A **`raster-dem`** source (your elevation tiles) | The DEM provides the raw height values. |
| **`maplibre-contour`** (npm package) | It runs a Web-Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts isolines, and serves them through a custom protocol (`contour://…`). |
| A **vector source** that points at that protocol URL | MapLibre can treat the generated contours exactly like any other vector tileset. |
| A **line (or symbol) layer** that draws the contours | The usual MapLibre style layer. |

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a complete example you can copy-paste into a sandbox.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# If you use a bundler (webpack, vite, parcel, etc.)
npm install maplibre-contour
# or, for a quick HTML demo, pull it from a CDN:
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

> **Tip:** `maplibre-contour` works with both **MapLibre GL JS** and **Mapbox GL JS** (the API is the same).  
> It ships a tiny Web-Worker that does the marching-squares / isoline extraction, so the main thread stays responsive.

---

## 2️⃣ Create the DEM source (the same one you would use for hillshade/terrain)

```js
// Example DEM – replace with your own tile URL/template
const demSource = new maplibregl.RasterDemSource({
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // The encoding must match the DEM you serve.
  // Common values: "terrarium", "mapbox", "raw"
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Optional: limit the max zoom you need for contours.
  maxzoom: 12
});
map.addSource('terrain-dem', demSource);
```

> **Why `encoding` matters** – the library reads the raw pixel values and converts them to meters. If you give the wrong encoding you’ll get wildly wrong elevations (or no contours at all).

---

## 3️⃣ Wire the library into MapLibre

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣ Create a DemSource object that knows how to read your DEM tiles.
const demContourSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // Same URL template you used for the raster-dem source.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // must match the DEM source
  maxzoom: 12,             // same as the DEM source
  // Set to true if you want the work done in a dedicated Web Worker.
  // This is recommended for any non-trivial zoom level.
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣ Register the helper with the map instance.
demContourSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

`setupMaplibre` registers a custom protocol handler (`contour://…`) that MapLibre can request just like a normal tile URL.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a **vector** source that points at the contour protocol

```js
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is generated by the helper.
  // You can pass options that control the contour spacing.
  tiles: [
    demContourSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing per zoom level (in meters) ──
      //   minor = thin lines, major = thicker / labelled lines
      thresholds: {
        // zoom : [minor, major]
        10: [200, 1000],
        11: [100, 500],
        12: [50, 200],
        13: [25, 100],
        14: [10, 50]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (meters)
      levelKey: 'level'      // 1 = major contour, 0 = minor
    })
  ],
  // The generated tiles are vector, so we need a maxzoom that covers
  // the highest zoom you’ll request.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**What the options do**

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `thresholds` | A map of zoom → `[minorSpacing, majorSpacing]`. The library will emit two sets of isolines per tile: the *minor* (thin) ones and the *major* (thicker) ones. |
| `elevationKey` | Name of the numeric property that holds the contour height (e.g., `ele`). |
| `levelKey` | Name of the property that tells you whether a line is a major contour (`1`) or a minor one (`0`). Useful for styling line width or adding labels. |
| `contourLayer` (optional) | If you want the generated vector tiles to be placed under a specific source-layer name, set this. By default the layer is called `"contours"` – you’ll reference that in `source-layer` later. |

---

## 5️⃣ Add a **line** layer (and optionally a **symbol** layer for labels)

```js
// 1️⃣ The contour lines themselves
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // The name of the source-layer inside the generated vector tiles.
  // If you didn’t set `contourLayer` above, it defaults to "contours".
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  paint: {
    // Thin lines for minor contours, thicker for majors.
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1,  // major
      2,  // minor (fallback)
      1   // default (should never hit)
    ],
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',   // major = darker
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'      // minor = lighter
    ]
  }
});

// 2️⃣ Optional: label the major contours
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major contours
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#000',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

**Styling tips**

* Use `line-dasharray` on the minor contours if you want a “dotted” look.
* Adjust `line-opacity` or `line-color` to blend nicely with your base map.
* If you have a dark basemap, invert the colors (light lines on dark background).

---

## 6️⃣ (Optional) Sync with other elevation layers

All of the following can share the **same** `raster-dem` source:

| Layer type | How to add |
|------------|------------|
| **Hillshade** | `type: "hillshade"` layer pointing at `terrain-dem`. |
| **Color-relief** | `type: "color-relief"` layer with an `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` expression. |
| **3-D terrain** | `map.setTerrain({ source: "terrain-dem", exaggeration: 1 })`. |
| **Contours** (this guide) | `type: "vector"` source + line/symbol layers. |

Because they all read the same DEM tiles, the browser only decodes each tile once, which keeps memory and CPU usage reasonable.

---

## 7️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (used later for terrain, hillshade, etc.)
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable 3-D terrain (optional, just to see the effect)
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 3️⃣ Set up maplibre-contour
    const demContour = new mlcontour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    demContour.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // 4️⃣ Add the vector source that points at the contour protocol
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demContour.contourProtocolUrl({
          thresholds: {10:[200,1000],11:[100,500],12:[50,200],13:[25,100],14:[10,50]},
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level'
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    // 5️⃣ Draw the contour lines
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 2,   // major = 2px
          1       // minor = 1px (fallback)
        ],
        'line-color': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',   // major
          'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'      // minor
        ]
      }
    });

    // 6️⃣ Optional: label major contours
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or paste it into a CodePen/JSFiddle) and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly as you pan/zoom. No pre-generated vector tiles are required.

---

## 📌 Common Pitfalls & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No contours appear** | Wrong `encoding` (e.g., DEM is `mapbox` but you told the library `terrarium`). | Verify the DEM’s encoding (check the source’s documentation) and set the same value on both the `raster-dem` source and the `DemSource`. |
| **Contours look jagged / missing at high zoom** | `maxzoom` on the DEM source is lower than the zoom you’re viewing. | Raise `maxzoom` on the DEM source (and on the `DemSource` config) to at least the highest zoom you need. |
| **Performance stalls when panning** | `worker: false` (contours computed on the main thread). | Use `worker: true` (default) so the heavy marching-squares runs off

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (roads, parks, labels, etc.) in MapLibre GL JS.

It uses the **ordinary `raster` source / `raster` layer** that MapLibre expects for image tiles.  
Because the tiles are already shaded, you **don’t add a `hillshade` or `raster-dem` source** – those are only for raw DEM data.

---

## 1️⃣  Create the raster source

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the raster source that points at your JPEG tiles
map.addSource('shaded-relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',                     // <-- ordinary image tiles
  tiles: [
    // Replace with the URL pattern you serve.
    // {z}/{x}/{y}.jpg works for most XYZ tile servers.
    'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,                      // most common; change if you use 512-px tiles
  // optional: if your tiles are in a different projection (e.g. 3857) you can set
  //   scheme: 'tms'   // for TMS Y-origin, otherwise leave out (default is XYZ)
  // optional: if you want the raster to fade in/out when zooming
  //   rasterFadeDuration: 300
});
```

> **Why `type: 'raster'`?**  
> A `raster-dem` source tells MapLibre “this is elevation data, treat it as a DEM”.  
> A plain `raster` source tells MapLibre “this is a picture that should be drawn as-is”.  
> Since your tiles are already a shaded image, `raster` is the correct choice.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a raster layer that draws the source

```js
// 2️⃣  Add the raster layer that actually paints the tiles
map.addLayer({
  id: 'shaded-relief-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',                     // <-- draws a raster source
  source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  paint: {
    // Optional: make the basemap a little transparent so you can see
    // underlying background colour or a faint grid.
    // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
  }
});
```

### Where to insert the layer

*If you are building the map from a **style JSON** that already contains a bunch of layers*, you need to make sure the basemap sits **below** everything else (except maybe a background layer). There are two ways:

| Method | Code snippet |
|--------|--------------|
| **Insert before the first non-background layer** (recommended) | ```js<br>const firstVectorLayerId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type !== 'background').id;<br>map.addLayer(basemapLayerObj, firstVectorLayerId);<br>``` |
| **Add after the background layer** (if you have one) | ```js<br>map.addLayer(basemapLayerObj, 'background');<br>``` |

If you are writing a **static style JSON**, just place the raster layer object **first** (after any `background` layer) in the `"layers"` array.

---

## 3️⃣  Keep your vector data on top

All of your vector sources (roads, parks, points, labels, etc.) should already be added as `fill`, `line`, `symbol`, etc. layers. Because the raster layer is now the **lowest** layer, those vector layers will automatically be drawn on top.

If you ever need to reorder a layer later, use:

```js
// Move a layer above the basemap (or any other layer)
map.moveLayer('my-vector-layer-id', 'shaded-relief-basemap-layer');
```

---

## 4️⃣  Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap + vector labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 0️⃣  Initialise the map (choose any centre/zoom you like)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources programmatically
      layers: [
        // Optional: a plain background colour (helps when the raster tiles
        // have transparent edges or you zoom out beyond tile coverage)
        { id: 'bg', type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Add the raster source (your pre-rendered shaded relief)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('shaded-relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: [
        // 👉  👉  👉  CHANGE THIS TO YOUR TILE URL TEMPLATE  👈  👈  👈
        'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Add the raster layer – put it right after the background
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const basemapLayer = {
      id: 'shaded-relief-basemap-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
      paint: {
        // 'raster-opacity': 0.95   // uncomment if you want a slight see-through
      }
    };

    // Insert just after the background layer (or before the first vector layer)
    const firstVector = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type !== 'background');
    const beforeId = firstVector ? firstVector.id : undefined; // undefined = append at end
    map.addLayer(basemapLayer, beforeId);

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add a sample vector source + label layer (replace with yours)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('countries', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'mapbox://mapbox.country-boundaries-v1'   // example – replace with your source
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'country-fills',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'countries',
      'source-layer': 'country_boundaries',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#ffffff66',
        'fill-outline-color': '#333333'
      }
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'country-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'countries',
      'source-layer': 'country_boundaries',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name_en'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#111111'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What this does

| Step | Result |
|------|--------|
| **Raster source** (`type: 'raster'`) | Loads your JPEG tiles exactly as they are. |
| **Raster layer** (`type: 'raster'`) | Draws the tiles on the canvas. |
| **Layer order** (`addLayer(..., beforeId)`) | Guarantees the basemap is the *lowest* visual layer, so all vector layers (including `symbol` label layers) appear on top. |
| **Vector layers** (`fill`, `line`, `symbol`, …) | Render normally, unaffected by the raster basemap. |

---

## 5️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Your JPEG tiles look gray/blank** | The source URL pattern is wrong, or the server returns 404. | Open one tile URL directly in a browser (`https://my.tileserver.com/relief/0/0/0.jpg`). |
| **Labels disappear under the raster** | The raster layer was added *after* the label layer, so it sits on top. | Re-order with `map.moveLayer('label-layer-id', 'shaded-relief-basemap-layer')` or add the raster layer *before* the label layer (see the `beforeId` argument). |
| **Tiles are blurry at high zoom** | Your raster tiles are only generated up to a certain maxzoom, but the map is requesting higher zooms. | Set `maxzoom` on the source (`maxzoom: 12`) and/or add a `fallback` style (e.g., a background colour) for zoom levels beyond that. |
| **You see a white/black “grid” between tiles** | The raster tiles have a different `tileSize` than the default (256) or the server returns empty tiles at the edges. | Match `tileSize` to the server (`tileSize: 512` if you serve 512-px tiles). |
| **You want the basemap to fade when zooming** | By default MapLibre fades raster tiles instantly. | Set `raster-fade-duration` in the layer’s `paint` object (e.g., `'raster-fade-duration': 300`). |
| **You need the basemap to be semi-transparent** | Opacity is still 1. | Add `'raster-opacity': 0.8` (or any value 0-1) in the `paint` block. |

---

## 6️⃣  Optional enhancements

| Goal | How |
|------|-----|
| **Add a subtle vignette or darkening at the map edges** | Add a `background` layer *after* the raster layer with a semi-transparent colour, then set its `background-opacity` to something like `0.1`. |
| **Switch basemaps on the fly** | Keep multiple raster sources (`relief-1`, `relief-2`) and toggle the visibility of their layers (`map.setLayoutProperty('relief-1-layer', 'visibility', 'none')`). |
| **Combine with a hillshade layer for extra depth** | You *can* stack a `hillshade` layer **on top of** the raster basemap (but *below* your vector data) if you want a faint 3-D shading effect. Use `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` and low `hillshade-exaggeration`. |
| **Add a sky/fog when you later enable 3-D terrain** | When you later call `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})`, also call `map.setSky({ ... })` so the horizon isn’t just the page background. |

---

## 7️⃣  TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

```js
// 1️⃣ source
map.addSource('relief', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256
});

// 2️⃣ layer – insert before the first non-background layer
const firstVector = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type !== 'background');
map.addLayer({
  id: 'relief-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'relief',
  paint: { /* optional raster-opacity, raster-fade-duration */ }
}, firstVector ? firstVector.id : undefined);
```

That’s it—your pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tiles now sit snugly beneath all of your vector data, with labels and other symbols rendered on top. 🎉

If you run into any specific error messages or need help integrating this into an existing style JSON, just drop the details and I’ll walk you through the exact adjustments. Happy mapping!
